CAT = "hs2Comms"; SEG = "COM"
Q = [
("When must an employer give health and safety training under the Management Regulations?",
 "On starting and when risks change", ["Only after an accident has happened", "Once every five years at most", "Only for staff still on probation"],
 "Regulation 13 requires training when people start and when they are exposed to new or increased risks, such as new equipment or new systems of work."),
("A warehouse brings in a new pallet truck. What should happen before staff use it?",
 "Training in how to use it", ["A new written safety policy", "A visit from an inspector", "A sticker saying it is safe"],
 "New equipment is one of the triggers for training. Staff need to know how to use it safely before they start."),
("Who pays for and gives the safety training an employee needs to do their job?",
 "The employer, in work time", ["The employee, at weekends", "The local college, by grant", "The insurer, after a claim"],
 "Training required by law is the employer's duty. It must be given during working hours and at no cost to the employee."),
("Why should training records be kept?",
 "To show who was trained and when", ["To set each worker's yearly pay rise", "To replace the risk assessments", "To meet a fire safety rule"],
 "Records show what training each person has had and when refresher training is due, and they help prove the employer met its duty."),
("What does it mean for a worker to be competent?",
 "Has the skills, knowledge and experience", ["Has worked at the firm the longest", "Holds a university degree in safety", "Has never had an accident at work"],
 "HSE describes competence as the combination of training, skills, experience and knowledge needed to do a task safely."),
("What colour is a mandatory safety sign, such as 'eye protection must be worn'?",
 "Blue circle", ["Yellow triangle", "Green rectangle", "Red circle with a bar"],
 "Mandatory signs are white symbols on a blue circle. They tell people what they must do."),
("A sign shows a black lightning bolt on a yellow triangle. What does it tell you?",
 "Danger of electricity", ["Switch off before leaving", "No electrical work allowed", "Electrical safety kit here"],
 "Yellow triangles with black edging are warning signs. With a lightning bolt, the warning is of electrical danger."),
("A white symbol on a green rectangle shows:",
 "A safe condition, such as an exit", ["An action you must carry out", "An activity that is banned", "A fire hazard close by"],
 "Green safe condition signs mark escape routes, emergency exits, first aid and eyewash stations."),
("A sign shows a lit cigarette inside a red circle with a red bar across it. What does it mean?",
 "No smoking", ["Smoking area this way", "Warning: fire risk", "Smoke alarm fitted"],
 "A red circle with a diagonal bar is a prohibition sign: whatever is pictured is not allowed."),
("A white symbol on a red square most likely marks:",
 "Firefighting equipment", ["An emergency exit route", "A first aid kit", "A no-entry area for visitors"],
 "Red is used for prohibition and for firefighting equipment, such as the location of a fire extinguisher or fire alarm call point."),
("Alternate yellow and black stripes on a floor edge or low beam warn of:",
 "Obstacles or a fall risk", ["A first aid point nearby", "A fire assembly point", "An eye wash station ahead"],
 "The Safety Signs Regulations use yellow and black (or red and white) stripes to mark places where people could collide with obstacles or fall."),
("When should safety signs be used?",
 "When risk remains after other controls", ["Instead of controlling the risk", "Only where members of the public visit", "Only when an inspector asks for them"],
 "Signs are needed where a significant risk cannot be avoided or adequately controlled by other means. They do not replace controls."),
("Which is the best way to give a new starter health and safety information they must remember?",
 "An induction with time for questions", ["A notice they might walk past", "A thick manual left on their desk", "A note slipped in with their first payslip"],
 "Two-way methods such as induction training let the employer check understanding; one-way notices are easily missed."),
("A machine has a laminated card fixed beside its controls. What is this card most likely for?",
 "Safe operating instructions", ["The operator's training record", "A page from the accident book", "The fire risk assessment"],
 "Operating instructions at the machine remind users of the safe way to start, use and stop it at the point where they need them."),
("A supervisor gathers the team for ten minutes at the start of a shift to talk about ladder use. This is:",
 "A toolbox talk", ["A disciplinary hearing", "A risk assessment", "A safety audit"],
 "Toolbox talks are short, informal briefings on one safety topic, held where the work is done."),
("Under the law, why must employers consult employees on health and safety?",
 "Workers know the risks of their jobs", ["To share out the employer's legal duty", "To pass the cost of controls on", "To let staff set their own rules"],
 "Consultation is required because workers often know the real hazards best. The employer still holds its legal duties."),
("In a workplace with a recognised trade union, who may the union appoint to represent workers on safety?",
 "Safety representatives", ["Fire marshals", "Trained first aiders", "Appointed persons"],
 "The Safety Representatives and Safety Committees Regulations 1977 let a recognised union appoint safety representatives."),
("Where no union is recognised, how can an employer consult staff on health and safety?",
 "Directly or via elected representatives", ["Only through the HSE", "By sending one annual email", "Through the firm's insurers"],
 "The Health and Safety (Consultation with Employees) Regulations 1996 allow consultation directly with staff or through elected representatives of employee safety."),
("A worker cannot read English well. How should the employer give them safety information?",
 "In a form they can understand", ["Only in writing, in English", "Not at all, to avoid confusion", "Only after six months' service"],
 "Information must be comprehensible to the person receiving it, for example with pictures, translation or a spoken briefing."),
("What should a data sheet supplied with a chemical be used for?",
 "Hazards and handling advice", ["Its price and delivery date", "The buyer's training records", "A guarantee that it is safe"],
 "A safety data sheet gives the hazards, safe handling, storage, first aid and spillage advice, and helps with the COSHH assessment."),
]
